"""
The identifier of one acceptance criterion, as written in the tag that
declares it.

Constrained here rather than checked in code, because this is the join
between three things that must agree: the criterion in a specification,
the citation a test carries, and the row a central store holds. The
pattern is the one `ac-traceability-standards.md` already fixes, minus
the leading `@` that makes it a Gherkin tag — the identifier is the
value, and the tag is only how it is written down.
"""
alias CriterionId: String ~= /^AC-[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9]{3}\.[0-9]+$/

"""
The slug that addresses one feature.

Deliberately unconstrained, for two reasons. The separator that composes
a hierarchical slug is not yet fixed, and a Strata regex literal cannot
contain a forward slash — so a pattern here could not express the
leading candidate even once it is chosen. Until the separator is
settled, the shape is stated and the check belongs to the CLI, which can
report a malformed slug with the position that makes it fixable.
"""
alias FeatureSlug: String

"""
A digest over a criterion's canonical form, written as `sha256:`
followed by lowercase hexadecimal.

What is hashed is the criterion's content and nothing else: its keyword
and name, each step's role and text, and each example row. Parser node
identifiers and source positions are excluded on purpose — node
identifiers are positional counters that shift whenever anything above
them is edited, and a criterion that moved has not changed.

This is what makes "which criteria changed since the last index" a
comparison of records rather than a diff of markdown.
"""
alias ContentHash: String ~= /^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}$/

"""
What a step contributes to its criterion.

Taken from the step's keyword rather than inferred from its text, so
that a consumer building a typed record gets precondition, trigger and
outcome as values instead of having to parse English. This is the whole
reason a parsed notation was adopted over prose: prose yields one
string, and this yields fields.
"""
enum StepRole {
  """A state or fact that holds before the trigger — a `Given` step."""
  Precondition: "precondition"

  """The action or event under test — a `When` step."""
  Trigger: "trigger"

  """The required result — a `Then` step."""
  Outcome: "outcome"

  """
  A step that continues whichever role preceded it — an `And` or `But`
  step.

  Recorded as its own role rather than resolved to the preceding one.
  The notation does not say which role a continuation belongs to, and
  guessing would put a fact in the index that the source does not
  contain.
  """
  Continuation: "continuation"

  """
  A step whose role the notation does not determine.

  Present so that an unrecognised keyword is representable. A record the
  model cannot hold is a record the index silently drops, and indexing
  never fails.
  """
  Unknown: "unknown"
}

"""
The level at which a criterion is intended to be verified.

Intent, not instruction. Nothing here executes a scenario or selects a
suite; the value records the judgement made when the criterion was
written, so that a coverage report can compare it against what tests
actually cite.
"""
enum VerificationLevel {
  """Verified against a unit in isolation."""
  Unit: "unit"

  """Verified against several units working together."""
  Integration: "integration"

  """Verified against the assembled system."""
  EndToEnd: "e2e"
}

"""
The notation a criterion was written in.

Recorded on every criterion rather than assumed for the corpus, because
the criterion notation is a replaceable input: prose criteria and
Gherkin criteria coexist for as long as any document still carries the
former, and a consumer that must treat them differently should branch on
a value rather than on which properties happen to be populated.
"""
enum CriterionNotation {
  """A Gherkin scenario, scenario outline, or tagged examples block."""
  Gherkin: "gherkin"

  """A prose acceptance criterion in a markdown list."""
  Prose: "prose"
}

"""
A feature written as a fenced Gherkin block inside a markdown document.
"""
struct FeatureSourceBlock {
  """
  The extent of the fenced block, opening and closing fences included.

  Recorded because the feature's own span covers the Gherkin inside the
  fence, and a caller that replaces or extracts the whole construct
  needs the fence too. This is the same distinction the framework's
  region scanner already draws between an inner and an outer extent.
  """
  block: Span
}

"""
A feature written as a standalone `.feature` file.

Carries nothing of its own. The file is the feature, so its path is
already on the feature and its extent is the whole document; this shape
exists so the source form is a value a consumer branches on rather than
one it infers from the absence of another property.
"""
struct FeatureSourceFile {
}

"""
Which admissible form a feature was written in.

The distinction survives into the index because it changes what a
consumer may do. A fenced block sits inside a document that owns
frontmatter, terminology and references, and rewriting it means editing
a region of markdown. A standalone file carries its own metadata in a
comment header and is the form external tooling ingests directly.
"""
@discriminator("form")
union FeatureSource {
  """A fenced Gherkin block within a markdown document."""
  @tag("fenced-block") FencedBlock: FeatureSourceBlock

  """A standalone Gherkin feature file."""
  @tag("feature-file") FeatureFile: FeatureSourceFile
}

"""
One step of one acceptance criterion.
"""
struct CriterionStep {
  """What this step contributes to the criterion."""
  role: StepRole

  """
  The keyword exactly as written — `Given`, `When`, `Then`, `And`,
  `But`, or a localized equivalent.

  Kept alongside the role because the role is a normalization and the
  keyword is what the author chose. A rewrite restores the author's
  word; a report quotes it.
  """
  keyword: String

  """The step's text, with the keyword and its trailing space removed."""
  text: String
}

"""
One cell of one example row, named by the column it sits under.
"""
struct ExampleValue {
  """The column name, taken from the example table's header row."""
  name: String

  """The cell's value, exactly as written."""
  value: String
}

"""
One concrete instance of a parameterized criterion.

A row rather than a sentence. Where a prose criterion carries its
example as unparseable text, this carries it as values a consumer can
query, which is most of what a parameterized criterion is worth.
"""
struct ExampleRow {
  """
  The row's cells, in the order the table's header declares them.

  A list of named values rather than a map. Column order is meaningful
  to a reader and map iteration order is not guaranteed, and a header
  that repeats a column name stays representable rather than losing a
  cell.
  """
  values: [ExampleValue]
}

"""
One acceptance criterion, extracted from whichever notation it was
written in.

A criterion is addressed by its identifier and nothing else. It is
recorded as a row of its own rather than nested inside the feature it
belongs to, for the reason the index already records references that
way: a consumer fetching one criterion by identifier reads one row, a
consumer listing a feature's criteria indexes this collection by
`feature_slug`, and merging two repositories' indexes into one graph is
appending rows rather than rewriting entries.
"""
struct Criterion {
  """The criterion's identifier, from the tag that declares it."""
  id: CriterionId

  """
  The slug of the feature this criterion belongs to.

  The slug rather than a position, so that a criterion survives its
  feature moving between documents or between the two admissible forms.
  """
  feature_slug: FeatureSlug

  """
  The path of the document the criterion is written in, relative to the
  repository root and written with forward slashes on every platform.

  Recorded even though the feature carries it too, so that a finding
  about a criterion cites a file without a join.
  """
  path: String

  """
  The criterion's name — the text following the `Scenario`,
  `Scenario Outline` or `Examples` keyword that declares it.
  """
  name: String

  """
  Rationale attached to this criterion alone, absent where none was
  written.

  Normative statements do not belong here; prose in this position is
  reported as a probable untagged criterion rather than recorded as
  requirement text.
  """
  description: String?

  """
  The name of the business rule this criterion sits beneath, absent
  where it sits directly under its feature.

  The rule's name is an assertion — "a build failure is the checker's
  job, not the indexer's" — so recording it puts the reason several
  criteria exist together in the index, scoped explicitly rather than
  inferred from position in a document.
  """
  rule: String?

  """
  Whether the criterion states a prohibition.

  Carried as its own property because a prohibition loses its
  grammatical marking in this notation: where prose wrote `SHALL NOT`,
  a scenario writes an ordinary sentence, and the fact that it forbids
  rather than requires would otherwise be unrecoverable from the record.
  """
  negative: Bool

  """
  Whether the criterion can only be verified by hand.

  A criterion marked this way is exempt from the obligation that some
  test cite it.
  """
  manual: Bool

  """
  The level at which the criterion is intended to be verified, absent
  where the author declared none.
  """
  level: VerificationLevel?

  """The criterion's steps, in the order they were written."""
  steps: [CriterionStep]

  """
  The criterion's concrete instances, empty where it is not
  parameterized.
  """
  examples: [ExampleRow]

  """The notation this criterion was written in."""
  notation: CriterionNotation

  """
  A hash of the criterion's content, excluding its position.

  Two indexes of the same criterion agree on this value whether or not
  the criterion moved; they disagree the moment its wording changes.
  """
  content_hash: ContentHash

  """
  Where in its document the criterion is written, as a span rather than
  a bare line number, so that a report and a rewrite both address the
  criterion itself.
  """
  position: Span
}

"""
One feature, extracted from a fenced Gherkin block or a standalone
feature file.

A feature is the unit a requirement maps onto: one cohesive capability,
independently testable, with its own slug. It is recorded as a row of
its own for the same reason its criteria are — a feature is addressable
without the document that happens to hold it, and moving one between
documents changes where it lives rather than what it is.
"""
struct Feature {
  """
  The feature's own slug, from the tag that declares it.

  This is the feature's identity. It does not change when the feature
  moves between documents or between the two admissible forms, which is
  what makes it a durable address.
  """
  slug: FeatureSlug

  """
  The feature slug's segments, outermost first, as the framework's
  separator divides it.

  Recorded rather than left to be re-derived. The separator is a
  framework convention, and a consumer merging indexes from several
  repositories — or querying every feature beneath a parent — should not
  have to know it in order to walk the hierarchy.

  A feature whose slug has no separator contributes a single segment.
  """
  segments: [String]

  """
  The slug of the document that owns this feature, from the tag that
  declares it.

  Ownership is stated rather than derived from the containing file,
  because a standalone feature file is not the document that owns it,
  and a feature must name its owner in either form.
  """
  document_slug: Slug

  """
  The path of the file the feature is written in, relative to the
  repository root and written with forward slashes on every platform.
  """
  path: String

  """The feature's name — the text following the `Feature` keyword."""
  name: String

  """
  The feature's description: its user story, and any design note or
  scope fence written beneath it.

  Absent where none was written, which is reported as a caution rather
  than a failure — a feature with no story is legible but poorer, and
  refusing to index it would help nobody.
  """
  description: String?

  """Which admissible form the feature was written in."""
  source: FeatureSource

  """
  Where in its file the feature is written, as a span covering the
  Gherkin itself.

  For a feature written as a fenced block, this covers the Gherkin
  inside the fence; the fence's own extent is carried by `source`.
  """
  position: Span
}
